  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the social services law, in relation to advising human\ntrafficking victims of the availability of social and legal services",
    "basePrintNo": "S924",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S924-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §483-cc, Soc Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Social Services Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S924\n\nSPONSOR: RAMOS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the social services law, in relation to advising human\ntrafficking victims of the availability of social and legal services\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe purpose of the bill is to help ensure that human trafficking survi-\nvors receive prompt access to social and legal services.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 provides that, upon encountering a person who reasonably\nappears to be or self-identifies as a victim of human trafficking, law\nenforcement personnel shall advise the victim of the availability of\nassistance from providers of social and legal services for human traf-\nficking victims. Unless the victim declines this assistance, the appro-\npriate provider will be contacted.\n\nSection 2 of the bill is the effective date.\n\n \nDIFFERENCE BETWEEN ORIGINAL AND AMENDED VERSION:\n\nThe amended bill adds language to promote effective communication with\nvictims of human trafficking who are not proficient in English and\nrequires social services districts to maintain and update a list of\nestablished providers of social and legal services to human trafficking\nvictims to be shared with law enforcement agencies and district attor-\nney's offices.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe bill provides that upon an encounter with a person who appears to\nbe, or identifies himself or herself as, a human trafficking victim,\npolice officers and district attorneys must provide the individual with\ninformation about available social and legal services. Further, unless\nthe victim asks that they not do so, these officials will contact a\nservice provider, who may then be present with the individual. This\nprocedure is similar to the current requirement that a rape crisis coun-\nselor be notified and made available to assist victims of sex offenses.\n\nSee Public Health Law section 2805-i (3); Executive Law section 642\n(2-a).\n\nHuman trafficking continues to be a major problem in New York and\nnationally. While NYS has enacted a number of important measures to\nassist human trafficking survivors, this bill takes that one step\nfurther to ensure that survivors will be able to promptly access needed\nsocial and legal services.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nMinimal, if any\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
